page("post-oak-mesquite-hickory-small-grill","Post Oak, Mesquite and Hickory on a Small Grill","Which Texas woods to burn in a small flat pack charcoal grill, how each one tastes, and how to use chunks and splits without bitter smoke.","full10",f"""
<p>Central Texas barbecue runs on post oak. South Texas and the border lean on mesquite. East Texas often uses hickory and pecan. All of them work in a small flat pack grill; the trick is scale. A firebox the size of a <a href="{COMPACT}">Slot Grill Compact</a> or <a href="{FULL}">Full Size</a> wants small pieces, burned down to coals, not log-sized splits throwing flames.</p>
{table(["Wood","Flavor","How to use it on a small grill"],[["Post oak","Clean, mild, the classic brisket smoke","Wrist-thick splits burned to coals, or chunks on charcoal"],["Mesquite","Strong, earthy, burns hot","Great for fast grilling (fajitas, steaks); go easy on long cooks, where it can turn harsh"],["Hickory","Bold, bacon-y","Chunks on charcoal for pork and sausage"],["Pecan","Sweeter, milder hickory cousin","Chicken, pork, ribs"],["Fruitwood (peach, apple)","Light and sweet","Poultry and fish"]])}
<h2>Charcoal base, wood for flavor</h2><p>The easiest Texas method on a small grill: a bed of lit lump charcoal for steady heat, and one fist-sized chunk of oak or hickory on the coals for smoke. Add another chunk when the smoke fades. You want thin, pale blue smoke. Thick white smoke means the wood is smoldering; give it more air or a smaller piece.</p>
<h2>All wood</h2><p>Both Slot Grill models burn cooking wood as well as charcoal. Burn splits down to coals first; see {L("burning-splits-down-to-coals","burning splits down to coals")}.</p>
<h2>Source it locally</h2><p>Buy or gather firewood close to where you burn it, use only dry, untreated hardwood, and follow any firewood rules at parks and leases.</p>
""","fire",[("Is mesquite too strong for barbecue?","Mesquite is excellent for fast, hot grilling. On long cooks it can get harsh, so many cooks use it sparingly or mix it with oak.")])

page("building-a-texas-coal-bed","Building a Texas Coal Bed in a Flat Pack Grill","How to light and lay out charcoal and wood coals in a small flat pack grill for searing, fajitas and slower beef cooks.","full13",f"""
<p>Everything on a lidless grill starts with how you lay the coals. Here are the four layouts Texas cooks use most on a small flat pack grill.</p>
{table(["Layout","How","For"],[["Full bed","Coals evenly across the firebox","Fajitas, smash burgers, quick steaks, sausage browning"],["Two-zone","Coals on two-thirds, last third empty","Steaks, chicken, links: sear then finish"],["Banked","Coals piled at one end only, food at the other","Indirect cooking: ribs sections, brisket flat stage one"],["Ring of fire","Coals around the edges, empty middle","A Dutch oven or a pan you want hot from the sides"]])}
<h2>Lighting</h2>{ol(["Light lump charcoal in a chimney with paper or natural fire starters; skip lighter fluid.","Wait for gray ash on the top coals, 15 to 20 minutes.","Pour into your layout with gloves.","Add a wood chunk if you want smoke.","Set the grates and preheat a few minutes."])}
<h2>How hot is it?</h2><p>The old hand test: hold your palm a few inches above the grate (carefully) and count. Pulling away in 2 seconds is very hot; 4 to 5 seconds is medium; 6 to 8 is low. It's rough, but it works with no gadgets. For meat, always trust an <a href="{THERMO}">instant-read thermometer</a>.</p>
""","fire",[])

page("managing-heat-without-a-lid","Managing Heat Without a Lid","How to cook Texas-style on an open flat pack grill: coal placement, fuel feeding, foil, pans and Dutch ovens in place of a lid.","full08",f"""
<p>A flat pack grill like the Slot Grill has no lid, so you can't trap heat the way an offset smoker or kettle does. That's not a flaw for grilling, where open heat is exactly right. For slower cooks, you borrow tools.</p>
<h2>Your heat controls</h2>{ul(["<b>Distance:</b> food over coals vs beside them.","<b>Quantity:</b> how many coals are lit.","<b>Spread:</b> piled tight is hotter, spread thin is cooler.","<b>Feeding:</b> adding lit coals keeps a steady temperature over hours.","<b>Containment:</b> foil wrap, a foil pan or a cast iron Dutch oven holds moisture and heat around tougher cuts."])}
<h2>Smoke first, then contain</h2><p>Smoke flavor mostly goes on early, while meat is cold and its surface is moist. So for ribs, brisket flats or chuck, smoke uncovered on the cool side for the first couple of hours, then wrap in foil or move into a Dutch oven to finish tender. That's the method behind {L("brisket-flat-on-a-small-grill","brisket flat on a small grill")} and {L("pork-spare-ribs-on-a-flat-pack-grill","ribs on a flat pack grill")}.</p>
<h2>Be honest about scale</h2><p>A whole packer brisket on a small lidless grill is not a good plan. Smaller cuts, sections of ribs, links, steaks, fajitas and birds are where a flat pack grill shines.</p>
""","fire",[("Can you smoke meat on a grill with no lid?","You can add real smoke flavor on the cool side of a lidless grill, then finish tough cuts wrapped or in a Dutch oven. It isn't the same as a closed smoker.")])

page("burning-splits-down-to-coals","Burning Splits Down to Coals","How to cook on wood coals in a small flat pack grill: split size, the burn-down, and keeping fresh coals coming.","full10",f"""
<p>Cooking on pure wood coals is old-school Texas, and a flat pack steel grill handles it fine; Slot Grill says both models burn charcoal or cooking wood.</p>
{ol(["Split wood small: thumb to wrist thick, short enough to lie in the firebox.","Start a small fire with kindling.","Add splits a couple at a time. Let flames burn down.","After roughly 30 to 45 minutes you'll have a bed of glowing coals.","Rake them where you need heat; keep one split burning at the far end as a coal factory.","Rake new coals over as the bed fades."])}
<h2>Tips</h2>{ul(["Dry wood only; wet wood smokes and sputters.","Don't cook over tall flames. You'll get soot and char, not barbecue.","Mesquite coals run very hot; great for fajitas."])}
""","fire",[])

page("burn-bans-and-texas-fire-rules","Burn Bans and Fire Rules for Grilling in Texas","How Texas county burn bans and park rules can affect charcoal grilling, and how to check before you light.","c28",f"""
<p>Texas counties can issue burn bans in dry conditions, and parks, lakes and beaches set their own fire rules. Whether a charcoal grill is allowed during a ban depends on the county order and the location. Rules change with the weather, so check every trip.</p>
<h2>How to check</h2>{ul(["Look up the current burn ban status for your county (the Texas A&amp;M Forest Service publishes a statewide burn ban map).","Read the county order. Many separate outdoor burning from cooking in a grill with a lid or enclosure; some don't. Don't guess.","Check park, lake, river outfitter or beach rules.","On private land and leases, ask the landowner."])}
<h2>Good habits on dry days</h2>{ul(["Grill on bare dirt, gravel, sand or concrete, never dry grass.","Keep water and a shovel handy.","Skip it on red-flag, high-wind days.","Drown ash and stir until cold."])}
<p>See also {L("texas-wind-and-heat-grilling","wind and summer heat")}.</p>
""","fire",[("Can I use a charcoal grill during a Texas burn ban?","It depends on the specific county order and the location. Read the current order and local park rules before lighting.")])

page("texas-wind-and-heat-grilling","Grilling in Texas Wind and Summer Heat","Handling coastal and plains wind, triple-digit afternoons and food safety in the heat when cooking on a small flat pack grill.","c08",f"""
<p>Wind and heat are the two Texas conditions that change a small open grill most.</p>
<h2>Wind</h2>{ul(["Wind fans coals hotter and burns them faster. Put the coal end into the wind and cook on the leeward side.","Shelter behind a truck or cooler, but keep safe clearance; never next to a vehicle's fuel area.","Bring more charcoal than you think.","Skip the fire on red-flag days."])}
<h2>Heat</h2>{ul(["Food safety: don't leave perishable food out more than 1 hour when it's above 90°F.","Keep coolers shaded and refill ice; keep raw meat sealed at the bottom.","Cook in the morning or evening when you can.","Hydrate, and wear closed shoes around coals."])}
""","fire",[("How long can food sit out in Texas heat?","Not more than 1 hour when it's above 90°F, per USDA guidance.")])
